  "query_preview": "..\n\nMain forecast concerns are on higher elevation snow, gusty winds \nthrough Monday, and chances for showers and a few thunderstorms.\n\nA trough continues to impact the western conus producing a more \nactive weather pattern across the forecast area. The cooler air \naloft will lower snow levels to around 8,000 ft through Monday. \nExpect accumulating snow for the higher elevations of the Crazy \nand Beartooth Mountains. 1 to 3 inches of snowfall is expected \nalong the Beartooth Pass, creating travel impacts. Other higher \nelevation locations could see 6 to 9 inches of accumulation, also\nlook for winds to gust as high as 40 mph and up to 55 mph on \nMonday which may produce some blowing snow at times. Those \nrecreating in the high country should be ready for winter-like \nconditions through Tuesday. \n\nExpect breezy to windy conditions over the western foothills \nmainly from late this afternoon through Monday when probabilities\nincrease to 50 to over 70 percent for wind gusts greater than 50 \nmph. The winds look to subside on Tuesday as the pressure gradient\nweakens. \n\nLow pressure lifts north across the region Monday into Tuesday but \npersistent shortwaves move over the area Wednesday through Friday \nwith continued low to medium chances for showers and thunderstorms. \nShear looks to be on the weaker side with the mid to upper level jet \nwell off to the northwest of the region. Warmer temperatures work \nback into the region with the majority of ECMWF ensemble members \nindicating highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s by Wednesday with \nhighs well into the 80s on Friday with some locations over eastern \nMontana potentially reaching to around 90. JW",
